// THE IDEAS INBOX. One JSON object a line, appended, never edited. Lines like
// this one are ignored, so these instructions live in the file they describe.
//
// Append with the CLI, which validates before it writes — a knob that does not
// exist is refused here rather than measured as a perfect null:
//
//   node scripts/cpu-lab.mjs idea --id pen-arm-cheaper \
//     --knobs '{"relieverDepthRate":0.5}' \
//     --note "the arms behind the closer at half a starter's rate" \
//     --room misty-fox-open \
//     --why "seat 3 paid $182 for a replacement arm two lots from the end" \
//     --by "room-analysis agent"
//
// Or write the line yourself:
//   {"id":"...","knobs":{"...":1},"note":"...","room":"...","why":"...","by":"..."}
//
// WHAT MAKES A GOOD ONE: a knob out of src/rules/cpuTunables.js (`cpu-lab.mjs
// list` prints every one with its shipped value), a note saying what it claims,
// and the room and the observation that made you think of it. The bench cannot
// tell a hunch from a reading, so the `why` is the only place the evidence
// survives — write it for someone who will read the result in a month and ask
// where the idea came from.
//
// WHAT HAPPENS NEXT: nothing you have to do. The queue reads this file, so the
// idea is a candidate from the next command onward, and the next `run` that has
// budget measures it across the whole grid.
{"id":"arm-balance-off","knobs":{"snakeBalanceBonus":0},"note":"drop the flat +35 an arm gets on top of its own need bonus","room":"fast-otter-grove","why":"snakeBalanceBonus fires on exactly the condition an arm's needBonus already fires on (pitcherNeed>0), so a needed arm is paid two need bonuses and a needed bat one. Zeroing it moves 22 of 90 computer turns across wise-robin-jersey/fast-otter-grove/sunny-trout-series and every single one swaps an arm for a bat (Nolan Ryan 730pt -> Vlad Guerrero 660pt at 2.1, Mark Mulder -> Jeff Kent, Freddy Garcia -> Junior Spivey). In those rooms the two seats that bought the most rotation finished last and next-to-last: the ace in fast-otter-grove put 2390 pts in four starters against a 2940-pt lineup and ran 44.4% while the human at 3990 pts of bats ran 55.2%.","by":"room-analysis agent (3 live snake rooms, 2026-11-11)","at":"2026-11-11"}
{"id":"scarcity-off","knobs":{"snakeScarcityRate":0},"note":"delete the snake positional scarcity term the auction deliberately does not have","room":"wise-robin-jersey","why":"The 2026-11-04 audit listed this as contradiction 1 and nobody has measured it; both queued scarcity candidates move the CAP, and the cap is not what binds. Across these three 3-team rooms the computers paid 83 scarcity bonuses averaging 13 pts, and snakeScarcityCap 120->60 moves ZERO of 90 turns while snakeScarcityRate 0 moves 18 (20%). In 57 of those 83 reaches (69%) the man being reached past was still on the board at that manager's next turn — Scott paid +65 for Whitey Ford past Kazuhisa Ishii, who lasted another three picks; Kasey paid +68 for Vinny Castilla past Troy Glaus, who lasted twenty-two. CAUTION for whoever reads the result: zeroing it is not a clean test of scarcity alone, because scarcity is the only counterweight the ace has to its own armLean — 12 of the 18 moved picks swap a bat for a starter.","by":"room-analysis agent (3 live snake rooms, 2026-11-11)","at":"2026-11-11"}
{"id":"pen-floor-by-room","knobs":{"relieverFloorByRoom":1},"note":"price the pen over the first arm the room will not take, not the worst arm dealt","room":"fast-otter-grove","why":"penPricedValue anchors the 2.5x top-reliever premium on the WORST reliever on the board, which is an auction fact (the night ends with the board stripped). These three rooms dealt 18 relievers to 3 managers with two pen seats each, so 12 arms were never in play, and the floor sat 46-84 model points under the first arm nobody took (114/88/64 against 172/172/110). At 2.5x that overprices every top arm by 1.5x the gap: Eric Gagne goes 236 raw -> 458, and he was the FIRST PICK OF THE DRAFT, ahead of Kevin Brown (358) and Barry Bonds (334); off a room-sized floor he is 331 and goes behind both. Five of the six computer bullpens in these rooms opened with a reliever inside the first five picks of the draft (#1 #2 #3 #4 #5) while the three humans waited until #13, #25 and #33, and the humans took two of the three rooms. Knob added dormant with this proposal: relieverFloorByRoom 0 is today's behavior, cpu-fingerprint identical on all 17 cells; 1 moves 8 of 90 computer turns in these rooms and every move is a round-1 closer giving way to a bat or an ace.","by":"room-analysis agent (3 live snake rooms, 2026-11-11)","at":"2026-11-11"}
// CHECK, 2026-11-11, on "pen-floor-by-room" above: it moves every seat at
// s4-classic / s4-long / s4-history, HALF the seats at s6-classic and s6-fiction,
// and is INERT at s8-classic. That is the mechanism, not a bug — the wider the
// room, the more of the board's relievers the pen seats actually reach, until the
// first arm nobody takes IS the worst arm dealt and the two floors coincide. So
// read its curve as a small-room fix and do not let an s8 null sink it. Live in
// a4-manual too (82/120 prices, mean $22.8), which is the one auction path that
// nominates through bestAutopickTarget.
{"id":"pool-premium-threshold-0-5","knobs":{"poolPremiumThreshold":0.5},"note":"bracket: poolPremiumThreshold at 0.5 instead of the shipped 0.6","why":"poolPremiumThreshold 0.8 -> 0.6 shipped at ef55c02 reading +0.42 +-0.12 at the last holdout, positive in all 11 auction cells. That says the top-of-deck lift matters and that 0.6 beats 0.8; it does not say 0.6 is the right percentile. 0.7 asks whether the gain is monotone or whether 0.6 overshot, 0.5 asks whether to keep going, and 0.8 is the reversal check.","by":"cpu-lab bracket","at":"2026-11-12"}
{"id":"pool-premium-threshold-0-7","knobs":{"poolPremiumThreshold":0.7},"note":"bracket: poolPremiumThreshold at 0.7 instead of the shipped 0.6","why":"poolPremiumThreshold 0.8 -> 0.6 shipped at ef55c02 reading +0.42 +-0.12 at the last holdout, positive in all 11 auction cells. That says the top-of-deck lift matters and that 0.6 beats 0.8; it does not say 0.6 is the right percentile. 0.7 asks whether the gain is monotone or whether 0.6 overshot, 0.5 asks whether to keep going, and 0.8 is the reversal check.","by":"cpu-lab bracket","at":"2026-11-12"}
{"id":"pool-premium-threshold-0-8","knobs":{"poolPremiumThreshold":0.8},"note":"the reversal: back to 0.8, the value poolPremiumThreshold shipped before. Must read the move that replaced it with the SIGN FLIPPED, or the bench is measuring the comparison and not the knob","why":"poolPremiumThreshold 0.8 -> 0.6 shipped at ef55c02 reading +0.42 +-0.12 at the last holdout, positive in all 11 auction cells. That says the top-of-deck lift matters and that 0.6 beats 0.8; it does not say 0.6 is the right percentile. 0.7 asks whether the gain is monotone or whether 0.6 overshot, 0.5 asks whether to keep going, and 0.8 is the reversal check.","by":"cpu-lab bracket","at":"2026-11-12"}
{"id":"arm-balance-parity","knobs":{"snakeBalanceBonus":60},"note":"pay an arm the same 60 for filling a staff hole that a bat already gets for filling a lineup hole, instead of 35","room":"cosmic-mouse-ballpark","why":"Decomposed the autopick score term by term at every computer's FIRST turn across five finished 15-pick snake rooms (srcx instrumentation of autopickScore; forcedNeed fires on every legal candidate at turn one, so it cancels). Before any opinion about any card enters, the board hands a BAT needBonus 152 + positionBonus 60 = 212, a STARTER 112 + balanceBonus 35 = 147, a RELIEVER 96 + 35 = 131. That is a flat 65-point structural tilt to bats on the turn when the board's best arms are still there, and it is two separate constants doing it: hitterPositionBonus returns 60 for a bat who fills a missing lineup spot (draft.js:4559) while the same structural fact in the staff pays snakeBalanceBonus 35 (draft.js:4454), and the need term counts HOLES OF THAT TYPE, which is 9/15 for bats against 4/15 for the rotation. The only thing that overturns it is armLean, which is +98 for the ace, 0 for balanced and purist, -21 for the slugger -- and the ace is the only persona that ever reaches a starter early: across 12 computer seats the aces took their first SP on own-turn 1,1,2,3,3,4 (6 of 6) while non-aces went 9,9,11,11 (4 of 5). The fifth is the control: in sunny-trout-series the balanced seat's best SP out-valued its best bat on the CARD by 322 to 205, beat the 65-point tilt, took Pedro on turn 1 -- and read 52.0 while the purist beside it (first SP on turn 9) read 42.8. In cosmic-mouse-ballpark the same balanced persona waited until turn 11, filled the rotation with the board's leftovers (Livan 0.059 / Reynolds 0.058 / Abbott 0.052 / Irabu 0.054 against the ace's -0.004 mean runsPerPa) and read 43.4 to the ace's 53.9 on RA/g 5.99 against 4.44. That standings pattern is a coincidence the bench has to test, not a result -- persona-winrate-is-field-dependent says a persona's placing is mostly field -- but the SAME persona sitting at both ends of it, tracking the turn it took its first arm, is what makes it worth an hour. 60 is the parity value, not a fishing dose, and the ladder confirms it is the targeted one: replayed over all 180 computer turns in the five rooms, 60 moves 26 of them and 20 of the 26 buy a STARTER; 85 moves 41 (26 SP / 15 RP); 140 moves 77 but 47 of those buy a RELIEVER, because the bonus is role-blind and the pen is the cheaper staff hole. Every flip at every dose is bat-to-arm, never the reverse. So read 60 first and only ladder up if it wins; a win at 140 would be a different claim.","by":"room analysis, 5 snake rooms","at":"2026-11-13"}
{"id":"need-span-flat","knobs":{"snakeNeedSpan":0},"note":"stop scaling a snake pick's need bonus by how many holes of that TYPE are left, so a rotation hole counts like a lineup hole","room":"cosmic-mouse-ballpark","why":"The other half of the bat tilt in arm-balance-parity, and an independent knob. needBonus is snakeNeedBonus + (holes of this card's type / turns left) * snakeNeedSpan, and the first term is provably dead weight: snakeNeedBonus=0 moves 0 of 180 computer turns across five finished snake rooms, because once forcedNeed holds the roster shape every legal candidate collects the same flat 80 and it cancels. All the life is in the span, and the span is what makes a lineup hole count more than a rotation hole -- at turn one of a 15-man room it pays a bat 9/15*120 = 72 against a starter's 4/15*120 = 32 and a reliever's 16. It is counting HOLES, not SUPPLY: these rooms deal 94 bats, 41 SP and 46 RP, and a 4-seat room seats 36 bats of the 94 (38%) and 16 starters of the 41 (39%), so bats and starters are equally scarce on the board while the need term weights bats 2.25x harder. Worse, the bias is front-loaded and self-cancelling: by a manager's sixth turn, with five bats in hand, bat-need and starter-need are both 4/10 and the term is a wash -- so it tilts hardest at exactly the turns when the top of the starter board is still there and goes quiet once it is gone. Replayed over all 180 computer turns, zeroing it moves 45 of them: 41 bat-to-arm, 3 arm-to-bat, 1 arm-to-arm, and the 41 split 22 to a STARTER and 20 to a reliever -- a far cleaner split than snakeBalanceBonus at any dose above parity, which runs to the pen. Raising it instead (240) moves 35, so the knob is live in both directions and 120 is not a corner. Measure this one WITH and AGAINST arm-balance-parity: they are two different constants pointing the same way, and if the parity fix wins, this one may be double-counting the same correction.","by":"room analysis, 5 snake rooms","at":"2026-11-13"}
// CHECK, 2026-11-12, on "arm-balance-parity" and "need-span-flat" above. All 39
// cells reproduce. Both move EVERY seat in 18 of the 19 snake cells, s2 through
// s8, and are INERT in all thirteen random-nomination auction cells — which is
// the mechanism, not a gap: both knobs are read only inside autopickScore, so
// the auction can only see them through manual nomination, and there they are
// live (parity a2 $72.5 / a3 $167.8 / a4 $95.8; span a2 $139.8 / a3 $106.8 /
// a4 $92.8). READ THE SNAKE CELLS AS PRIMARY: a3-manual moves 43 of 78 prices
// by a mean of $168 because changing what gets NOMINATED cascades through the
// whole night's bidding, so a win there is not evidence about the autopicker.
// The one snake exception is s2-long-fiction (half the seats for parity, inert
// for the span): a 2-seat 20-man room has more turns than holes, so the need
// terms stop binding. need-span-flat has the wider snake reach of the two
// (parity drops to 6/8 at s4-fiction), so if both read positive the span is the
// likelier double-count.
// COUNTER-EXAMPLE, 2026-11-13, room fair-alpaca-slider, on "arm-balance-parity"
// and "need-span-flat" above. READ THEM OFF THE DOSE CURVE, NOT THE MIXED
// COLUMN. The rooms those ideas came from had TWO reaching seats in a 4-seat
// room, which is what stripped the arm board before the waiting seat's turn.
// fair-alpaca-slider is the natural experiment with only ONE: 3 seats, one ace,
// one purist, one human. Every seat took its first starter LATE (own-turn 5, 6,
// 6) and nobody paid for it — rotations bunched at 0.0229 / 0.0288 / 0.0420 mean
// runsPerPa and both computers beat the human (52.5 / 51.5 / 46.0). The starter
// cliff is only a little shallower at 3 seats than 4 (best to last-drafted arm
// 0.052-0.062 against 0.064-0.087), so the moderator is not board width on its
// own: it is HOW MANY RIVALS ARE ALSO REACHING. That is exactly the shape
// league-duels-measure-denial exists to catch, so if parity wins at k=1 and
// decays to nothing as the table adopts it, it is denial and must be rejected,
// however good the mechanism looks. The mechanism is still visible here: at
// snakeBalanceBonus 60 the purist takes Greg Maddux '03 (580pt SP, two rotation
// holes open) instead of ERIC OWENS '03, a 110-point centre fielder ranked 46th
// on his own model — the least defensible pick in the six rooms read so far.
// Six-room totals now, 210 computer turns: 60 moves 34 (26 to a starter, 8 to a
// reliever, 0 to a bat), 140 moves 92 but 54 go to the pen, 0 moves 41 all to a
// bat, snakeNeedSpan=0 moves 55 (30 SP / 22 RP / 3 bat). snakeNeedBonus=0 still
// moves 0 OF 210 — six rooms, provably dead weight, deletable.
// RETRACTION, 2026-11-13, room nimble-seal-maple, correcting the COUNTER-EXAMPLE
// comment directly above. That comment said fair-alpaca-slider's flat standings
// showed the moderator is "HOW MANY RIVALS ARE ALSO REACHING". That explanation
// is WRONG, and nimble-seal-maple falsifies it: 3 seats, one reaching computer,
// the same shape as alpaca — and here the purist took its first starter on own
// turn 5 and read 52.8 while the slugger waited to turn 11, drafted the exact
// nine-bats-first shape BBBBBBBBBRSSSSR, and read 49.1 on a rotation 0.027
// runsPerPa worse. The real reading is simpler: alpaca had NO TREATMENT. Its
// three seats took their first starter on turns 5, 6, 6 — a one-turn spread, so
// there was no late seat to punish. Normalised by the turn gap, alpaca pays the
// same rate as everywhere else. Pooled over seven rooms, 45 turns of delay
// between each room's earliest and latest first-starter seat: 0.2209 rpa, or
// 0.0049 RUNS PER PA PER TURN OF WAITING, with per-room rates 0.0032 to 0.0104
// and alpaca at 0.0059, dead centre. Deepest starter cliffs are cosmic 0.087 and
// seal 0.089 and the two shallowest rooms are still 0.052, so board width is not
// the moderator either. WHAT STANDS from that comment is only the general
// practice, which holds for any CPU change and not because of alpaca: read these
// off the DOSE curve, not the mixed column, or denial reads as a gain
// (league-duels-measure-denial). Seven-room totals, 240 computer turns:
// snakeBalanceBonus 60 moves 40 (31 to a starter, 9 to a reliever, 0 to a bat),
// 140 moves 105 but 60 go to the pen, 0 moves 46 all to a bat, snakeNeedSpan=0
// moves 61 (36 SP / 22 RP / 3 bat), snakeNeedBonus=0 still moves 0 OF 240.
// CORRECTION, 2026-11-13, room peachy-wolf-field, on the WHY fields of
// "arm-balance-parity" and "need-span-flat" above. THOSE WHY FIELDS OVERSELL
// THE STANDINGS EVIDENCE AND SHOULD BE READ WITH THIS LINE. Eight finished
// snake rooms, 26 seats, 18 of them computers, each roster simulated 3000
// seasons: first-starter turn against rotation quality is r = +0.735, but
// first-starter turn against WIN% is only r = -0.239, and rotation runsPerPa
// against win% r = -0.322 (computers only; every seat reads +0.743 / -0.298 /
// -0.304). So the MECHANISM is confirmed and the PAYOFF is not. Waiting
// reliably buys worse arms -- pooled, 0.0047 runs per PA per turn of delay over
// 51 turns -- and that does not reliably buy fewer wins. peachy-wolf-field is
// the sharpest case: the balanced seat took its first starter on own turn 9,
// finished with the WORST rotation in the room (0.0597) and WON at 53.8, while
// the slugger reached on turn 3, had the better rotation, and came last at 43.9
// on the room's worst offence (RF/g 4.36 against 4.91). This is the exact shape
// of pitching-spend-is-an-outcome, where seven rooms gave corr($pitching,win%)
// = +0.65 and lifting starter bids still read -0.02 ABSOLUTE. So do not read
// these two ideas as pre-confirmed. What justifies measuring them is not the
// standings but the inconsistency: positionBonus pays a bat 60 and
// snakeBalanceBonus pays an arm 35 for the identical structural fact, and
// nobody chose that gap. A null here is a perfectly good answer.
// PREDICTION ON THE RECORD, 2026-11-13, room wild-moose-valley, on
// "arm-balance-parity" and "need-span-flat" above. I NOW EXPECT BOTH TO READ
// ZERO OR NEGATIVE, and that is a reversal of the WHY fields written two days
// ago. wild-moose-valley is the within-persona experiment the earlier rooms
// could not supply: BOTH computers drew `balanced`, same board, same two
// rivals, only the seeded weight wobble between them -- and they split hard on
// the very thing these ideas move. Kasey reached on own-turn 3 (Warren Spahn
// '04, the board's best arm) and finished with a rotation 0.0203 runsPerPa
// better; Scott waited until turn 10 and filled it with arms his own model
// ranked 29th, 31st, 31st and 32nd. Five sim seeds, 20000 seasons each:
//   Scott  waited to turn 10, rotation 0.0361, 47.21 +-0.38
//   Kasey  reached on turn  3, rotation 0.0158, 45.86 +-0.67
// Reaching cost 1.35 win points at roughly 2 sigma. The exchange is visible in
// the box score: Kasey bought 0.34 RA/g of prevention and handed back 0.29 RF/g
// of offence, which is run-prevention-wins-snake's null bat/arm exchange rate
// showing up in one room. Pooled over nine rooms and 20 computer seats, the
// mechanism correlation is unmoved at r = +0.734 (first-starter turn against
// rotation quality) while the payoff correlation keeps shrinking as rooms
// arrive: -0.239 at eight rooms, -0.183 now. CAVEATS: one room, one pair, and
// perturbation moves all their weights, not only their arm appetite, so this is
// the best natural experiment available and not a controlled swap; it is also a
// within-league read, so it ranks these two rosters against each other and a
// shared human rival rather than measuring either absolutely. STILL WORTH THE
// RUN: the 60-against-35 inconsistency is real and unchosen, the dose curve is
// what settles it, and a clean null that agrees with this prediction is a
// result worth having written down.
